WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.080
[MUSIC]
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:09.975
Ken thanks a lot for for coming out here
we really appreciate it and

00:00:09.975 --> 00:00:12.316
I know a lot of people are looking forward
to this.

00:00:12.316 --> 00:00:15.946
Just to pick up on Gart's introduction you
know, you're one of

00:00:15.946 --> 00:00:21.000
the few CEO's on Wall Street who's still
at the helm post the financial crisis.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:24.200
In addition, it couldn't have been easy,
you know, just months after becoming CEO,

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:27.340
being hit with 9/11, and facing those
challenges.

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:31.152
Through all those, what have you learned
about leadership through crisis?

00:00:31.152 --> 00:00:34.484
I know there lessons that are applicable
about, to day to day leadership?

00:00:34.484 --> 00:00:43.720
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think clearly my tenure has
been one where I've had to confront

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:50.660
some of the most challenging crisis that
we've seen in the last 100 years.

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:55.090
And one of the mottos that I follow
actually comes from Napoleon.

00:00:56.230 --> 00:01:01.770
And it's frankly a motto that I think
about frequently, and

00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:04.970
it's very simple but very hard to execute,
and

00:01:04.970 --> 00:01:09.520
that is that the role of a leader is to
define reality and give hope.

00:01:10.850 --> 00:01:15.700
And that's what I think about, frankly,
everyday, because if in

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:21.050
fact you wanna have an inspired workforce,
you have to,

00:01:21.050 --> 00:01:25.730
in fact, make sure that people understand
that you're grounded in reality.

00:01:25.730 --> 00:01:29.560
But then you have to give them the
strategies, the ideas, and

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:33.870
the values that give them reasons to be
hopeful.

00:01:33.870 --> 00:01:40.540
And as we went through the 9/11 crisis,
certainly we went through

00:01:41.970 --> 00:01:48.100
the crisis of the this downturn.

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:51.790
One of the things that I recognized is
that it was very,

00:01:51.790 --> 00:01:55.840
very important, very quickly to analyze
and

00:01:55.840 --> 00:02:01.350
then communicate what the reality of the
situation was.

00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:06.330
And even if that meant that those facts
were harsh,

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:12.260
the organization needed to understand it,
investors need to understand that.

00:02:13.470 --> 00:02:17.770
But second, very importantly, it was
critical to, in fact,

00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:22.880
take what appeared to be a very confusing
situation and, and

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:27.360
it, this is not meant to overly simplify
problems.

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:32.020
But what I do believe is you've gotta give
people an organizing device.

00:02:33.140 --> 00:02:40.650
And so in the case of the challenges of
2008 and 2009

00:02:42.190 --> 00:02:46.920
I had a mantra that I really believe if
you asked almost any company in

00:02:48.110 --> 00:02:52.760
around the world for us any employee, what
was the mantra?

00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:56.560
What they would say is it's stay liquid,
cuz that was part of the reality.

00:02:56.560 --> 00:02:58.950
Commercial markets were frozen,

00:02:58.950 --> 00:03:03.050
the credit markets were frozen, I thought
our balance sheet was strong, but

00:03:03.050 --> 00:03:07.310
I wanted to make people understand based
on what was happening in the financial

00:03:07.310 --> 00:03:10.690
services marketplace was stay liquid, what
did that mean?

00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:15.760
Stay profitable, and that was part of one
giving hope to the organization that

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:20.670
despite the fact that a number of
financial services companies were losing

00:03:20.670 --> 00:03:27.570
money, we in fact had an opportunity to
distinguish ourselves.

00:03:27.570 --> 00:03:31.940
If we could be consistently profitable
every quarter, which we were.

00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:36.210
And the third, back to the hope was that
were still going to

00:03:36.210 --> 00:03:39.320
selectively invest in growth.

00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:41.730
And gave them very spec,

00:03:41.730 --> 00:03:45.130
very specific examples of the areas that
we were going to invest in.

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:47.880
&gt;&gt; Just to follow, pick up on that.

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:52.460
How do you, in the midst of what looks
like a complete perfect storm,

00:03:52.460 --> 00:03:56.410
get people inspired to drive for growth
and invest in that and take those risks?

00:03:56.410 --> 00:04:02.630
&gt;&gt; I think part of what you have to do,
again, as you state what the reality is,

00:04:02.630 --> 00:04:05.950
I'll give you some examples from our, from
our business.

00:04:05.950 --> 00:04:10.640
You gotta give people some markers of, how
do they judge you making progress?

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:14.490
Because simply to go out to the
organization and say look, we're, we're

00:04:14.490 --> 00:04:19.580
faced with an incredible crisis, one of
the worst financial crises in 100 years.

00:04:19.580 --> 00:04:23.225
But trust me, I think I'm smart, I think
I'm energetic.

00:04:23.225 --> 00:04:25.300
[LAUGH] We're gonna get throgh this.

00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:27.280
Your gonna say, give me a break.

00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:29.180
that's, that's not gonna work.

00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:34.430
So what I tried to do is one, I said,
let's look at the history of our company.

00:04:34.430 --> 00:04:36.540
American Express is 160 year old company.

00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:42.160
I actually used a number of examples
through our history.

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:46.460
But in fact, was able to use some of the
examples from 9/11.

00:04:46.460 --> 00:04:51.340
Because most investors had written us off
in a number of ways.

00:04:52.420 --> 00:04:56.090
And I took 'em through, how we were able
to get through that.

00:04:56.090 --> 00:04:59.458
But then I said, here are some markers
that we are going to use.

00:04:59.458 --> 00:05:05.840
One is, are we going to be able to
maintain

00:05:07.270 --> 00:05:12.900
the premium prices that we charge for our
card, and to our merchants?

00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:16.090
Because, as you saw in the crisis with the
downturn, and

00:05:16.090 --> 00:05:18.720
the precipitous drop in spending.

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:25.220
So in our case, from 2002 to 2007, we
averaged 16% growth in billings.

00:05:26.510 --> 00:05:33.680
Which really lead the market and five
quarters into the,

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:40.015
five quarters in, or five months into the
downturn, we had declined in spending 16%.

00:05:40.015 --> 00:05:43.070
16% negative growth in Billings.

00:05:43.070 --> 00:05:46.980
People were saying where the bottom is
falling out,

00:05:46.980 --> 00:05:48.450
credit losses were increasing.

00:05:49.500 --> 00:05:55.510
So I said, one is, one what we want to
make sure is,

00:05:55.510 --> 00:05:59.640
will customers pull their card out of
their wallet at the same time.

00:05:59.640 --> 00:06:01.870
So what's the transaction level?

00:06:01.870 --> 00:06:04.470
And frankly, part of the risk there, was
saying to people.

00:06:04.470 --> 00:06:06.040
But that's part of the reality is,

00:06:06.040 --> 00:06:09.990
here's how you can judge if we're gonna
have success or failure.

00:06:09.990 --> 00:06:12.080
So, people are pulling their card out of
their wallet.

00:06:13.590 --> 00:06:17.300
At basically the same rate that they did
pre-downturn.

00:06:17.300 --> 00:06:19.320
And that should give you confidence in the
future.

00:06:19.320 --> 00:06:22.270
So even though billings had dropped 16%.

00:06:22.270 --> 00:06:24.645
The loyalty to the product is they're
pulling that card out.

00:06:24.645 --> 00:06:27.830
And that was one, and that was one that we
tracked and

00:06:27.830 --> 00:06:31.380
talked about to the organization on a
consistent basis.

00:06:31.380 --> 00:06:34.100
And there were frankly some people
concerns, saying Ken,

00:06:34.100 --> 00:06:37.360
if we give some of this out, what if we
don't achieve it.

00:06:37.360 --> 00:06:38.420
So that's part of the reality,

00:06:38.420 --> 00:06:41.370
then we're gonna have to come up with
strategies to ensure.

00:06:41.370 --> 00:06:43.590
That we can get people to pull their card
out of their wallet more.

00:06:44.690 --> 00:06:49.180
Second thing we looked at is we said, will
people trade down because as

00:06:49.180 --> 00:06:52.260
you saw with a lot of retailers, people
were trading down.

00:06:52.260 --> 00:06:57.320
Our high price centurion products which in
fact when you first get a card,

00:06:57.320 --> 00:07:02.550
you have to pay a 5000 dollar fee, and
then a 25 dollar fee every year.

00:07:02.550 --> 00:07:07.050
The affluent sec, segment was hit hard,
and we said, can we hold on to that?

00:07:07.050 --> 00:07:09.520
Well, one [UNKNOWN] we were pleased about,
but

00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:14.150
we held that out as a metric, is that
people kept their cards.

00:07:14.150 --> 00:07:15.510
They did not trade down.

00:07:15.510 --> 00:07:22.520
And so, so our, our retention levels,
really stayed, in very, very good shape.

00:07:24.050 --> 00:07:27.530
Third area, which was an important one
again, stay liquid.

00:07:27.530 --> 00:07:30.230
Is it was at certain points in time,

00:07:30.230 --> 00:07:37.800
an absolutely horrifying experience when
the markets froze up.

00:07:37.800 --> 00:07:39.660
And people were very concerned about
funding.

00:07:40.870 --> 00:07:46.490
So what we said at this point was that we
in fact having.

00:07:46.490 --> 00:07:51.010
Limited to no experience in the deposit
gathering business,

00:07:51.010 --> 00:07:56.440
that we actually were going to focus on
gathering deposits,

00:07:56.440 --> 00:07:59.400
cuz we were rel, very reliant on the
wholesale funding model.

00:08:01.260 --> 00:08:01.760
&gt;&gt; Hm.

00:08:02.770 --> 00:08:07.540
&gt;&gt; This is where I think a brand is very
important, and fortunately the service.

00:08:07.540 --> 00:08:10.890
Ethic that the dean talked about, the
reputation and

00:08:10.890 --> 00:08:14.710
the trust that was developed was helpful
to us, but

00:08:14.710 --> 00:08:20.570
we put together a very innovative strategy
and from a standing start in 12

00:08:20.570 --> 00:08:26.350
months we in fact had gathered over 26
billion dollars in deposits.

00:08:26.350 --> 00:08:28.260
Taking care of our funding needs.

00:08:28.260 --> 00:08:32.720
That in fact helped the organization a
great deal.

00:08:32.720 --> 00:08:35.340
Then because of the uncertainty of the
environment,

00:08:35.340 --> 00:08:39.370
not because we thought we were gonna go
out of business, but

00:08:39.370 --> 00:08:43.340
frankly I said as an insurance policy,
should we do TARP?

00:08:43.340 --> 00:08:45.800
And we went back and forth with that.

00:08:45.800 --> 00:08:49.700
And we ended up taking close to three
billion dollars of Tarp money

00:08:49.700 --> 00:08:50.970
which by the way we were.

00:08:50.970 --> 00:08:56.340
We were one of the first companies to
repay at a 26% return for the U.S.

00:08:56.340 --> 00:08:58.610
taxpayer which I feel very good about.

00:08:58.610 --> 00:09:02.900
I also remind people when they talk about
Tarp is I

00:09:02.900 --> 00:09:05.970
said I say I don't know about your
investment portfolio.

00:09:05.970 --> 00:09:10.920
But I don't think you got a 26% return
[LAUGH] on your investment portfolio.

00:09:10.920 --> 00:09:16.670
So the, the important point there was to
take some contingencies out, and

00:09:16.670 --> 00:09:20.320
to explain to people the reasons why.

00:09:20.320 --> 00:09:22.740
And then, in fact, we.

00:09:22.740 --> 00:09:28.290
Identified areas for investment, so I
think the balances, you've gotta demon,

00:09:28.290 --> 00:09:32.280
demonstrate the strategies, but then you
have to set some markers that you

00:09:32.280 --> 00:09:35.160
can let people know about the progress on
a very regular basis.

00:09:36.190 --> 00:09:39.990
&gt;&gt; As you deliver those messages, how do
you get those messages out to the broader

00:09:39.990 --> 00:09:44.300
organization, and then what are some tools
or tactics you use to actually pulse your

00:09:44.300 --> 00:09:46.980
organization so you're, you're not
isolated in the executive suite?

00:09:46.980 --> 00:09:48.610
&gt;&gt; Well, it's a good ,.

00:09:48.610 --> 00:09:53.820
We, we do a lot of, pulsing through the
organ, organization.

00:09:55.340 --> 00:09:59.370
There's obviously a lot of informal
feedback, but what we have done,

00:09:59.370 --> 00:10:02.450
increasingly, is to rely online.

00:10:02.450 --> 00:10:09.450
On, on getting that feedback, because the
terrific thing is you can.

00:10:09.450 --> 00:10:12.050
Get questions out very quickly.

00:10:12.050 --> 00:10:16.160
We're an organization that knows how to
analyze information and

00:10:16.160 --> 00:10:19.160
data, that's one of our strengths.

00:10:19.160 --> 00:10:24.770
And so to be able to correlate, some of
the drivers of satisfaction,

00:10:24.770 --> 00:10:29.320
where people are confused on an issue, is
absolutely critical.

00:10:31.310 --> 00:10:35.120
We have town halls via satellite.

00:10:35.120 --> 00:10:41.150
Because I think you want to have a notion
that we are one company

00:10:41.150 --> 00:10:46.390
and doing these global town halls have
been very important.

00:10:47.870 --> 00:10:52.050
I do also as well as a number of our
senior leaders.

00:10:52.050 --> 00:10:54.000
It's what I call brown bag lunches.

00:10:55.340 --> 00:10:59.340
With approximately thirty employees.

00:10:59.340 --> 00:11:03.640
Where I give them first sort of a three
minute here's my temperature.

00:11:03.640 --> 00:11:05.550
Here's how I feel about the business.

00:11:05.550 --> 00:11:07.500
Then ask me anything you want.

00:11:07.500 --> 00:11:10.510
And then I say to them always at the end
of the lunch.

00:11:10.510 --> 00:11:11.280
Only have one favor.

00:11:12.300 --> 00:11:17.010
Is I would like you to communicate to five
to ten people.

00:11:17.010 --> 00:11:18.920
And give them your impression.

00:11:18.920 --> 00:11:20.890
And you can say, I think Ken is a jerk.

00:11:20.890 --> 00:11:22.970
I don't know what he was talking about.

00:11:22.970 --> 00:11:24.360
It's better that they hear it from you.

00:11:25.690 --> 00:11:28.020
Then they just imagine it.

00:11:28.020 --> 00:11:30.970
It would be preferable I don't say that to
them.

00:11:30.970 --> 00:11:31.985
They are more positive.

00:11:31.985 --> 00:11:33.120
[LAUGH].

00:11:33.120 --> 00:11:36.370
and, and those have gone, those have gone
very well.

00:11:36.370 --> 00:11:40.690
But, I think, basically, in the world we
live in today just like marketing

00:11:49.440 --> 00:11:50.414
every single channel, you should be using.

00:11:50.414 --> 00:11:51.024
face-to-face, online, every single
channel.

00:11:51.024 --> 00:11:52.760
Speaker: I've read, one of the things you,

00:11:52.760 --> 00:11:56.478
you encourage with an AMEX is this idea of
constructive confrontation.

00:11:56.478 --> 00:11:57.940
Speaker: Right.
Speaker: I'm just wondering what it

00:11:57.940 --> 00:12:00.535
means for you, and if there are any costs
associated with that.

00:12:00.535 --> 00:12:02.230
Speaker: Yeah.
Here's, here's what's important.

00:12:02.230 --> 00:12:04.160
And I think, you know, it's critical.

00:12:04.160 --> 00:12:11.830
What you're trying to develop in any
organization, is a level of transparency.

00:12:11.830 --> 00:12:14.650
And openness and honesty.

00:12:14.650 --> 00:12:19.040
And what I find in most organizations
that,

00:12:20.900 --> 00:12:25.900
where things get bogged down, is when
people feel that they have to

00:12:25.900 --> 00:12:30.010
game the system in some way to get a point
across.

00:12:30.010 --> 00:12:35.700
The amount of time you waste, the chances
for, misalignment.

00:12:35.700 --> 00:12:40.870
Of, priorities, is a real problem.

00:12:40.870 --> 00:12:44.170
And, so what, what I try to do.

00:12:45.640 --> 00:12:52.696
Is to make sure that I leverage

00:12:52.696 --> 00:12:56.959
the information that we have about the
organization.

00:12:58.470 --> 00:13:03.080
That people clearly understand what our
priorities are.

00:13:04.250 --> 00:13:05.680
And I think that works very well.

00:13:08.010 --> 00:13:09.250
&gt;&gt; Switching gears a little bit,

00:13:09.250 --> 00:13:13.650
you've been at American Express since
1981, which I find incredible just

00:13:13.650 --> 00:13:17.870
given the world I see today at least where
employees switch companies a lot.

00:13:17.870 --> 00:13:19.840
What has kept you at AMEX over all these
years?

00:13:19.840 --> 00:13:23.800
And how has your leadership style evolved
over the years to where it is today?

00:13:23.800 --> 00:13:26.460
&gt;&gt; You know, I think what has kept me at
the company, and I'll

00:13:26.460 --> 00:13:30.610
give you just a little bit of background,
in a sense is I, I went to law school.

00:13:32.470 --> 00:13:34.940
Thinking that I would probably practice
for two or

00:13:34.940 --> 00:13:38.010
three years and then I really wanted to go
into venture capital.

00:13:39.670 --> 00:13:42.860
And this is really going to date me,

00:13:42.860 --> 00:13:48.025
but when I was coming out cable was really
the big deal.

00:13:48.025 --> 00:13:52.495
[LAUGH] That was going to change the
world.

00:13:52.495 --> 00:13:53.730
[LAUGH]

00:13:53.730 --> 00:13:59.190
&gt;&gt; And so, Amex had entered into a joint
venture with Warner Amex,

00:14:00.220 --> 00:14:06.550
and at the time, Lou Gerstner was running,
one of the major parts of the company,

00:14:06.550 --> 00:14:13.570
and, Lou had heard about me from a client
that, he knew.

00:14:13.570 --> 00:14:17.040
And I talked to him about joining the
planning department in

00:14:17.040 --> 00:14:19.240
a relatively junior role.

00:14:19.240 --> 00:14:21.180
But I said I really want to work on cable.

00:14:22.400 --> 00:14:25.710
And he said we'll see if we can make that
happen.

00:14:27.740 --> 00:14:32.950
And I've told this tory in the past and so
Louis heard me say this.

00:14:32.950 --> 00:14:33.655
It never happened.

00:14:33.655 --> 00:14:38.379
[LAUGH] but, but it didn't happen for some
very good reasons.

00:14:38.379 --> 00:14:44.513
And one of the things that was a
particularly important

00:14:44.513 --> 00:14:50.910
point that Lou made to me was that, that
AmEx was a company

00:14:50.910 --> 00:14:57.465
that was a service company, but was a very
idea-driven.

00:14:57.465 --> 00:14:58.666
Company.

00:14:58.666 --> 00:15:02.378
And that you could, in fact, see your
ideas implemented in

00:15:02.378 --> 00:15:07.070
a relatively short period of time, and you
could impact people's lives.

00:15:08.370 --> 00:15:11.330
And for me that was one of the important
things that I

00:15:11.330 --> 00:15:15.590
believe in business is I wanna make a
difference in people's lives.

00:15:15.590 --> 00:15:16.668
I like being in the service business.

00:15:16.668 --> 00:15:17.640
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:15:17.640 --> 00:15:23.310
&gt;&gt; But I also like the intellectual
challenge that you can come up with ideas,

00:15:23.310 --> 00:15:24.470
and you can make things happen.

00:15:26.000 --> 00:15:34.410
And so, this is a company that continues
to evolve in some very major ways.

00:15:34.410 --> 00:15:39.735
And is a dramatically different company,
because when I joined the company in

00:15:39.735 --> 00:15:46.040
1981 it was on the verge of actually
becoming a financial supermarket.

00:15:46.040 --> 00:15:49.593
We had Lehman, we had Shearson, if we had
all those companies today,

00:15:49.593 --> 00:15:51.108
I would not be talking to you.

00:15:51.108 --> 00:15:52.130
[LAUGH].

00:15:52.130 --> 00:15:53.830
&gt;&gt; It would be a very different
environment.

00:15:53.830 --> 00:15:59.669
So I think ideas, things that has kept me
there, ideas make a big difference.

00:16:02.480 --> 00:16:06.550
The service commitment that people have is
incredible, and

00:16:06.550 --> 00:16:11.900
I often say where the company has made
strategic mistakes.

00:16:12.920 --> 00:16:16.250
What has protected the company has been
our

00:16:16.250 --> 00:16:19.910
front line people cuz they really have
built the reputation of the company.

00:16:22.320 --> 00:16:26.170
&gt;&gt; A number of CEOs who've spoken here in
the past have remarked that reflection and

00:16:26.170 --> 00:16:29.740
introspection and setting aside time and
space to think and

00:16:29.740 --> 00:16:31.500
generate ideas is important.

00:16:31.500 --> 00:16:33.530
And it has been important to their
leadership style.

00:16:33.530 --> 00:16:35.480
Just wondering how you feel about that and

00:16:35.480 --> 00:16:38.860
what are things that you do to kind of set
that time and space for yourself?

00:16:38.860 --> 00:16:44.080
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I, you know, I think what is
important and what's critical is to make

00:16:44.080 --> 00:16:51.050
sure that you are exposing yourself to a
range of ideas.

00:16:51.050 --> 00:16:53.392
So you had talked about constructive
confrontation.

00:16:53.392 --> 00:16:54.705
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:16:55.780 --> 00:16:59.280
&gt;&gt; Which is I think I didn't answer as
directly as I could, and

00:16:59.280 --> 00:17:02.130
this will, will actually be helpful there.

00:17:03.500 --> 00:17:07.820
I think what is important and why
constructive confrontation is so

00:17:07.820 --> 00:17:10.810
important, and why it opens up ideas,

00:17:10.810 --> 00:17:15.090
is because the status quo has to be
constantly challenged.

00:17:16.720 --> 00:17:19.529
And what you want is an environment,

00:17:19.529 --> 00:17:23.970
what I want is an environment where people
really feel passionate.

00:17:23.970 --> 00:17:27.360
And if you feel passionate about things,
you should debate them, and

00:17:27.360 --> 00:17:28.720
you should care about them, and

00:17:28.720 --> 00:17:34.470
you should challenge each other and that
makes for an incredibly vital environment.

00:17:34.470 --> 00:17:39.945
So, I will sometimes instigate debate
intentionally.

00:17:39.945 --> 00:17:43.870
And go in and frankly, take a position and

00:17:43.870 --> 00:17:47.970
a point of view that I know will screw
things up.

00:17:47.970 --> 00:17:51.877
Now you got to be careful, because when
you're CEO, they'll go, what, wow!

00:17:51.877 --> 00:17:53.660
[LAUGH] You know, what's the deal?

00:17:53.660 --> 00:17:56.000
So you have to be careful in doing that.

00:17:56.000 --> 00:17:59.750
But, what is critical is there are a lot
of

00:17:59.750 --> 00:18:03.350
terrific ideas at every level of a
company.

00:18:03.350 --> 00:18:08.580
And so you wanna set up the mechanisms
again, through every channel to do that.

00:18:08.580 --> 00:18:13.530
But the tone has to be set at the top that
you, in fact,

00:18:13.530 --> 00:18:19.440
are the type of person that people feel
comfortable arguing with you on issues.

00:18:20.730 --> 00:18:25.690
I always then make the point, however,
that once we decide on a course of action,

00:18:26.880 --> 00:18:30.660
we can't then have people continuing to
debate, we gotta move forward.

00:18:31.710 --> 00:18:37.430
Then the other thing I try to do is to
reach outside the company,

00:18:37.430 --> 00:18:42.970
and and I put together I think a pretty
worldwide network

00:18:44.470 --> 00:18:51.087
of people that I check in with a fair
amount online.

00:18:51.087 --> 00:18:55.313
Some of it on the phone, some of it in
person.

00:18:55.313 --> 00:19:01.280
That really bring out some different
perspectives.

00:19:01.280 --> 00:19:05.210
So, it may not be that they give you the
brilliant idea, but they give you

00:19:05.210 --> 00:19:09.128
a different way of thinking an issue that
helps you come up with that idea.

00:19:09.128 --> 00:19:10.410
&gt;&gt; Sure.

00:19:10.410 --> 00:19:13.450
I've, based on other places where you've
spoken,

00:19:13.450 --> 00:19:15.380
you commented that mentoring is important
to you.

00:19:15.380 --> 00:19:18.260
And I was just wondering if you'd speak to
who, who've been some of

00:19:18.260 --> 00:19:22.120
your mentors along the way, and who
inspires you as a leader today?

00:19:22.120 --> 00:19:23.340
&gt;&gt; Good.

00:19:23.340 --> 00:19:27.390
I'll, I'll, talk about two or three
mentors that I'll have and then the other

00:19:27.390 --> 00:19:31.480
point I'll make about mentoring, cuz I
think it's sometimes is misunderstood.

00:19:32.790 --> 00:19:35.560
What I really believe is that if you wanna
develop,

00:19:35.560 --> 00:19:39.020
to develop a true mentor relationship.

00:19:40.510 --> 00:19:44.730
And you wanna network, you wanna network
off of your performance.

00:19:46.480 --> 00:19:51.910
Just in fact, to meet someone is generally
not

00:19:51.910 --> 00:19:56.680
that helpful because, here's sorta how the
trade works, at least for me.

00:19:56.680 --> 00:20:01.630
Is if I'm gonna take on a mentee, and I
really do like to help people and

00:20:01.630 --> 00:20:02.740
work with people and develop.

00:20:02.740 --> 00:20:06.500
I think it's one of the most satisfying
parts of leadership.

00:20:06.500 --> 00:20:08.390
I wanna get something in return.

00:20:09.600 --> 00:20:15.079
I wanna be stimulated by the ideas that
they can come up with in the dialogue.

00:20:15.079 --> 00:20:20.510
I want in fact, for them to have the
commitment,

00:20:21.560 --> 00:20:26.710
that they're gonna really improve in the
areas that they need to improve on and

00:20:26.710 --> 00:20:28.700
that they're capable of really making
things happen.

00:20:29.750 --> 00:20:32.720
that's, that's, that's part of the trade
off.

00:20:32.720 --> 00:20:37.270
And I think when you look at a mentor at
different levels,

00:20:37.270 --> 00:20:42.670
sometimes you're looking for someone who
can give you technical advice.

00:20:44.000 --> 00:20:44.698
That's not a mentor, to me.

00:20:44.698 --> 00:20:46.290
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:20:46.290 --> 00:20:49.890
&gt;&gt; That's an expert and that's very
important to seek out that expert advice.

00:20:50.940 --> 00:20:54.250
What I look for in mentors, is really,
judgement.

00:20:54.250 --> 00:20:56.270
because, that, to me, at the end of the
day,

00:20:56.270 --> 00:21:00.370
from a leadership standpoint, is, is, is
really the toughest challenge.

00:21:01.830 --> 00:21:06.969
and, so, over the years, I've had a
diversity of mentors.

00:21:09.220 --> 00:21:12.240
And I'll talk about them in two
categories.

00:21:13.830 --> 00:21:19.410
One is there's the obvious senior mentor.

00:21:19.410 --> 00:21:26.080
And a few of the people that I would put
in that category for me is Warren Buffet.

00:21:27.220 --> 00:21:30.940
Is a mentor I've been fortunate that he is
our largest investor.

00:21:32.130 --> 00:21:37.050
But we've also developed a very good
professional and personal relationship.

00:21:38.450 --> 00:21:45.755
What I know with Warren is that
performance matters, a big deal.

00:21:45.755 --> 00:21:48.510
[LAUGH] He's a very nice man,

00:21:48.510 --> 00:21:52.398
but do not think at all that performance
is not what it's about.

00:21:52.398 --> 00:21:58.790
But, but what is most impressive with
Warren for

00:21:58.790 --> 00:22:04.970
me is this combination of his intellect
and his values.

00:22:06.020 --> 00:22:12.110
And how he approaches an issue and how he
has a criteria

00:22:12.110 --> 00:22:15.590
that really is one of balanced of someone
who wants to

00:22:15.590 --> 00:22:21.840
be incredibly successful, do very well in
his investments.

00:22:21.840 --> 00:22:25.320
But at the same time, he really wants to
do the right thing.

00:22:27.340 --> 00:22:31.870
And how he approaches issues have been
impressive.

00:22:31.870 --> 00:22:38.900
Early on in my career, Lou Gersner was, a
very important mentor to me.

00:22:38.900 --> 00:22:41.649
Hard driving, rigorous.

00:22:42.750 --> 00:22:47.890
Objective and I really learned a lot from
him.

00:22:47.890 --> 00:22:54.470
And Harvey Gollum was someone who

00:22:54.470 --> 00:23:01.481
had the ability to analyze issues and.

00:23:01.481 --> 00:23:08.210
Really drive down through the details, and

00:23:08.210 --> 00:23:12.740
never assume because someone was telling
him a view, one of Harvey's favorite,

00:23:14.100 --> 00:23:18.370
comments was always, is that a fact, or is
that your perspective?

00:23:19.820 --> 00:23:24.740
And that just is a useful discipline, cuz
sometimes people get the two confused.

00:23:25.740 --> 00:23:32.260
So I've really tried to pick different
qualities from the mentors.

00:23:32.260 --> 00:23:36.240
The other part of mentorship is, that is
important though is, I've had a lot of

00:23:36.240 --> 00:23:43.100
mentors in a structural sense that have
been at lower levels in the company.

00:23:44.350 --> 00:23:48.870
And and I also make this point that what
you get with a,

00:23:48.870 --> 00:23:54.120
a mentor that's a lower level, is at a
lower level in a company is an unfiltered

00:23:54.120 --> 00:23:59.720
perspective in the sense that they'll tell
you who the phonies are, in the company.

00:24:01.220 --> 00:24:03.080
And really is amazing, cuz they've been
there,

00:24:03.080 --> 00:24:08.940
cuz people take liberties with people that
they don't think are senior, and and

00:24:08.940 --> 00:24:12.820
I really have developed relationships with
people, cuz again,

00:24:12.820 --> 00:24:16.610
remember my criteria for a mentor is
advice and judgement.

00:24:16.610 --> 00:24:19.850
And there are people at lower levels who
have incredible judgement and

00:24:19.850 --> 00:24:21.340
incredible insight.

00:24:21.340 --> 00:24:26.440
That, coming up through the company helped
me tremendously to say you know,

00:24:26.440 --> 00:24:29.200
at the end of the day, here are some
people you should stay away from,

00:24:29.200 --> 00:24:31.990
because you can't trust them, for these
reasons.

00:24:31.990 --> 00:24:35.590
Seen them in these situations or watch out
for these situation.

00:24:35.590 --> 00:24:38.770
Or you may be approaching an issue in this
way.

00:24:38.770 --> 00:24:40.560
Here's another way to think about it.

00:24:40.560 --> 00:24:43.450
And so, I really encourage people

00:24:43.450 --> 00:24:48.910
to develop mentors at different levels of
the organization.

00:24:48.910 --> 00:24:50.170
&gt;&gt; Great.

00:24:50.170 --> 00:24:51.520
Before we open it up to the audience.

00:24:51.520 --> 00:24:52.690
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; I actually had a personal question,

00:24:52.690 --> 00:24:53.950
if you don't mind.

00:24:53.950 --> 00:24:56.700
Unemployed MBA student, I was wondering if
you'd help me qualify for

00:24:56.700 --> 00:24:57.545
the centurion guard?

00:24:57.545 --> 00:24:58.835
[LAUGH].

00:25:00.810 --> 00:25:01.955
&gt;&gt; My shameless ask.

00:25:01.955 --> 00:25:03.440
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I can, I can help.

00:25:04.490 --> 00:25:05.218
[LAUGH] But

00:25:05.218 --> 00:25:09.310
unless you meet the criteria, I don't
think I'm gonna have much of an impact.

00:25:09.310 --> 00:25:14.020
[LAUGH] but, I can advise you on how you
can perform

00:25:14.020 --> 00:25:18.320
to certainly receive [LAUGH] [UNKNOWN]
&gt;&gt; I'll take you up on that.

00:25:18.320 --> 00:25:22.479
&gt;&gt; Yes.
[APPLAUSE].

00:25:22.479 --> 00:25:28.120
With that we'd love to open it up,

00:25:28.120 --> 00:25:32.868
I believe there are mics along, or they're
already planted.

00:25:32.868 --> 00:25:38.961
[INAUDIBLE]

00:25:38.961 --> 00:25:43.912
&gt;&gt; Mr Schnell, thank you for your.

00:25:43.912 --> 00:25:47.040
&gt;&gt; Son.
&gt;&gt; Thank you for your speech just had

00:25:47.040 --> 00:25:51.160
a quick question about Amex is undoubtedly
known as kind of the flag bearer within

00:25:51.160 --> 00:25:56.460
the financial services industry when it
comes to card member satisfaction and,

00:25:56.460 --> 00:26:01.340
and premier customer servicing, so my
question is kind of.

00:26:01.340 --> 00:26:05.800
Moving forward when it comes to this,
this, premier servicing and

00:26:05.800 --> 00:26:09.820
satisfaction what are your thoughts on
sorta of the next wave and

00:26:09.820 --> 00:26:13.400
what is, what is AMEX doing to kinda get
ahead of the curve?

00:26:13.400 --> 00:26:18.640
Is the you know, traditional centurion
platinum style of servicing going

00:26:18.640 --> 00:26:22.260
to be relevant to sorta the next wave of
high net worth individuals and

00:26:22.260 --> 00:26:24.270
how do you get ahead of that curve?

00:26:24.270 --> 00:26:26.270
&gt;&gt; Yeah, here's what's important,
important, ,.

00:26:27.450 --> 00:26:30.860
as, as you look at our company cuz we have
been

00:26:31.930 --> 00:26:36.080
traditionally identified with what we call
the issuing business.

00:26:36.080 --> 00:26:38.670
That we issue a range of cards.

00:26:38.670 --> 00:26:44.860
The reality is that service is important
in every area,

00:26:44.860 --> 00:26:51.320
but what you have to do is segment that
value proposition of service in,

00:26:51.320 --> 00:26:53.810
in a very customized way.

00:26:53.810 --> 00:26:56.280
And that's what we are doing.

00:26:56.280 --> 00:26:58.770
However whenever you look at our company,
and

00:26:58.770 --> 00:27:03.370
I'll talk about just a few of the areas
that I think are very exciting going

00:27:03.370 --> 00:27:07.330
forward, we are more than just a payments
company.

00:27:07.330 --> 00:27:10.440
If you said, can what really is your
company about?

00:27:11.440 --> 00:27:18.030
What we do is we facilitate value to a
range of segments

00:27:18.030 --> 00:27:25.410
using the payments vehicle, but what we're
not is a commodity payments company,

00:27:25.410 --> 00:27:31.440
so I'll just give you a few examples that
will illustrate this point.

00:27:31.440 --> 00:27:36.290
One is if you look at the payments
landscape, and you say, who's on,

00:27:36.290 --> 00:27:39.610
who's in the issuing business, we are in
the issuing business.

00:27:39.610 --> 00:27:40.840
In terms of billings,

00:27:40.840 --> 00:27:47.203
we're the largest issuer in the world $620
billion in billings last year.

00:27:47.203 --> 00:27:52.680
However, we also are a network processor
similar to Visa and

00:27:52.680 --> 00:27:56.150
MasterCard, and we're a global network
processor.

00:27:56.150 --> 00:28:00.540
But our card member, which helps our value
proposition with merchants,

00:28:02.170 --> 00:28:08.010
spends three to four times the size of
average spend of Visa and MasterCard.

00:28:08.010 --> 00:28:13.890
The third piece that we're on is that we
actually acquire merchants which Visa and

00:28:13.890 --> 00:28:14.720
MasterCard don't.

00:28:16.330 --> 00:28:19.370
Fourth piece is in rewards and

00:28:19.370 --> 00:28:25.400
loyalty, we are actually one of the
largest loyalty companies in the world.

00:28:25.400 --> 00:28:30.972
Now, the fact that we have information,
unlike any other person in payments,

00:28:30.972 --> 00:28:33.930
company in payments, we have information
and

00:28:33.930 --> 00:28:36.490
data on the issuing side of the business.

00:28:36.490 --> 00:28:40.060
And we have information and data on the
merchant side of the business.

00:28:41.580 --> 00:28:43.790
Information, as we all know, is power.

00:28:44.960 --> 00:28:49.981
So as we go forward, and this is some of
the things that we are doing now,

00:28:49.981 --> 00:28:54.951
is one, business to business represents a
tremendous opportunity.

00:28:54.951 --> 00:28:59.740
And we're one of the leading payment
companies in business to business.

00:28:59.740 --> 00:29:02.260
But the value proposition we're offering
companies is,

00:29:02.260 --> 00:29:05.240
we're just not saying we're going to
facilitate your transaction.

00:29:05.240 --> 00:29:06.300
We're saying to them,

00:29:06.300 --> 00:29:12.660
we actually can help you reduce your
expenses using our products and services.

00:29:12.660 --> 00:29:17.880
And so we have entered ventures which
concur in a range of companies.

00:29:17.880 --> 00:29:22.780
Because if part of what we can do, is
facilitate that value to drive it.

00:29:22.780 --> 00:29:25.020
We think that presents substantial
opportunity.

00:29:26.130 --> 00:29:31.490
Emerging payments, we made an acquisition
of a company called Revolution Money.

00:29:33.030 --> 00:29:37.530
That is, has some similar capabilities to
PayPal.

00:29:37.530 --> 00:29:40.460
Because I believe person to person is
gonna be important,

00:29:40.460 --> 00:29:44.494
I believe mobile is gonna be very
important.

00:29:44.494 --> 00:29:49.720
We are doing a deal right now with ICBC,
the largest bank in China, and

00:29:49.720 --> 00:29:56.030
UNPay, the largest mobile provider, where
we have a mobile payments capability.

00:29:56.030 --> 00:30:02.036
But what we're increasingly doing is using
our information management capabilities-

00:30:02.036 --> 00:30:05.339
To drive additional value for our
customers.

00:30:05.339 --> 00:30:07.457
So a few things that might surprise you,

00:30:07.457 --> 00:30:11.460
Darden Restaurants is one of the largest
restaurant chains in the country.

00:30:12.580 --> 00:30:16.880
They rely heavily on our data to cite
their,

00:30:16.880 --> 00:30:19.720
their restaurants that they're gonna put
up.

00:30:19.720 --> 00:30:20.320
Why?

00:30:20.320 --> 00:30:24.000
Because we have a range of data of
spending, which we aggregate.

00:30:24.000 --> 00:30:27.730
We don't violate privacy laws, but we're
using that information.

00:30:27.730 --> 00:30:30.030
They, they pay us a monthly fee.

00:30:30.030 --> 00:30:34.300
That is a very attractive area, we're
doing that for more and more companies.

00:30:34.300 --> 00:30:38.560
We have a deal with AT&amp;T Wireless because
our customers travel all over the world,

00:30:38.560 --> 00:30:43.070
so you connect the modeling with that with
roaming opportunities, and that's major.

00:30:43.070 --> 00:30:48.230
If you go on the Delta Airlines site now,
for their loyalty program,

00:30:48.230 --> 00:30:54.200
the company that is managing the site, the
marketing and the merchandising, is us.

00:30:55.240 --> 00:31:01.690
Now, the important part of that, from a
virtual circle standpoint, is that

00:31:01.690 --> 00:31:09.730
we in fact, believe that the opportunity
with our information to connect online and

00:31:09.730 --> 00:31:15.400
offline is tremendous, because we not only
know habits and preferences and

00:31:15.400 --> 00:31:19.100
sites where people go, but we actually
know if the transaction was consummated.

00:31:20.980 --> 00:31:26.230
So emerging payments and alternative
payments is gonna be important for

00:31:26.230 --> 00:31:31.540
us because we now have what I call
horizontal scale across the payments

00:31:31.540 --> 00:31:35.420
landscape that is gonna help us drive that
business.

00:31:35.420 --> 00:31:41.690
So what I see for us, is that increasingly
you're gonna see a melding of what we're

00:31:41.690 --> 00:31:47.540
doing and what I call the broad category
of servicing, in a range of businesses,

00:31:47.540 --> 00:31:52.969
that will help facilitate value for a
number of online and offline companies.

00:31:54.350 --> 00:32:00.530
And we're going to leverage and optimize
the data we have more and the and

00:32:00.530 --> 00:32:07.700
the partnerships, and our customer
connections more than ever.

00:32:07.700 --> 00:32:13.360
However, the hallmark has to be, that I
believe particularly

00:32:13.360 --> 00:32:17.400
in the uncertain environment that we're
in, and you look at the importance of

00:32:17.400 --> 00:32:22.100
reputation, is that trust is gonna be even
more important, and

00:32:22.100 --> 00:32:26.040
we know it's even gonna be more important
online, and that's where service comes in.

00:32:29.780 --> 00:32:31.900
&gt;&gt; Thank you for sharing your time with us
today.

00:32:31.900 --> 00:32:34.810
You've remarked that leadership need to be
consistently worked on in

00:32:34.810 --> 00:32:37.630
order to attain the followership needed to
be successful.

00:32:39.110 --> 00:32:41.780
You cited at the time improving your
listening skills.

00:32:41.780 --> 00:32:45.680
What are you working on now, and what
would you say is the most important skill

00:32:45.680 --> 00:32:50.280
for us to develop while at GSB in light of
the changing economy?

00:32:50.280 --> 00:32:51.620
&gt;&gt; Here's, here's, here's what I would
say.

00:32:51.620 --> 00:32:55.020
I think, first I'll go with, what are the
important skills, then I'll tell you

00:32:55.020 --> 00:32:59.510
what I'm working on which is always a
range of things, and I really do believe,

00:32:59.510 --> 00:33:05.770
if you wanna be a strong leader, you gotta
constantly work on areas.

00:33:05.770 --> 00:33:09.070
I think that, what is absolutely critical
for

00:33:09.070 --> 00:33:12.810
a leader today is the ability to connect
the dots.

00:33:15.200 --> 00:33:18.260
You can't remain alone in your discipline.

00:33:19.520 --> 00:33:22.950
What you gotta understand is how thing
interrelate.

00:33:22.950 --> 00:33:26.590
What are the connections, and

00:33:26.590 --> 00:33:30.800
then what are the relevant value drivers
for your business?

00:33:31.910 --> 00:33:39.660
So the ability to in fact take in a broad
set of facts and knowledge,

00:33:40.740 --> 00:33:47.140
and to see what the connection is to your
particular business is critical.

00:33:48.460 --> 00:33:53.520
And so that means at the end of the day,
awareness of what is

00:33:53.520 --> 00:33:57.760
going on in the world and trends, and
constantly saying,

00:33:59.250 --> 00:34:03.620
what does this development mean for my
business and my customer?

00:34:05.430 --> 00:34:10.210
Because at the end of the day, if you
wanna be a market leader and

00:34:10.210 --> 00:34:14.630
innovate, you actually have to come to
conclusions that most people don't.

00:34:16.000 --> 00:34:18.820
And most people don't because they take a
conventional view

00:34:19.860 --> 00:34:20.980
of what they're working on.

00:34:20.980 --> 00:34:24.060
So that ability to connect is critical.

00:34:24.060 --> 00:34:30.943
For me what I'm particularly focused

00:34:30.943 --> 00:34:36.640
on now is the BRIC countries.

00:34:36.640 --> 00:34:42.840
And part of what I'm doing from a
development standpoint is while I'm

00:34:42.840 --> 00:34:47.330
learning a lot about the business issues,
I'm actually spending a fair amount of

00:34:47.330 --> 00:34:53.500
time through history books, trying to
understand some of the culture and

00:34:53.500 --> 00:34:59.470
the psychology in the markets that we're
operating in, because I think

00:34:59.470 --> 00:35:06.150
that it is easier to have a view of some
of the technical issues,

00:35:06.150 --> 00:35:10.110
but if you want the type of emotional
engagement.

00:35:10.110 --> 00:35:15.310
And one of the things that I think is
critical in the marketing of

00:35:15.310 --> 00:35:21.350
products is this balance be, between
rational value and emotional connection.

00:35:22.600 --> 00:35:28.960
And if in fact people really believe that
I understand who you are, and

00:35:28.960 --> 00:35:35.410
I actually respect how you think, and that
can make an incredible difference.

00:35:35.410 --> 00:35:41.380
So that's one of the areas that I'm
working on.

00:35:41.380 --> 00:35:43.810
&gt;&gt; Yeah thank you very much for your talk.

00:35:43.810 --> 00:35:45.150
My name is Kristoff Freezee.

00:35:45.150 --> 00:35:46.822
I'm an MBA one.

00:35:46.822 --> 00:35:53.440
You draw this picture of the great
investor you'll have who wants to do well,

00:35:53.440 --> 00:35:55.719
and at the same time, right.

00:35:55.719 --> 00:35:59.367
Can you, from your point of view, give us,
or

00:35:59.367 --> 00:36:04.250
elaborate on, how do you define you are
doing right?

00:36:04.250 --> 00:36:07.110
You are in an, in an industry where trust
is important.

00:36:07.110 --> 00:36:11.580
You mentioned the data and sensitive data
you have, and where as the CEO,

00:36:11.580 --> 00:36:17.500
you actually draw the line in your
company, and be this person that says,

00:36:17.500 --> 00:36:20.200
we can't do this because it's not right.

00:36:20.200 --> 00:36:25.046
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I, I think one of the most

00:36:25.046 --> 00:36:30.750
critical responsibilities of

00:36:30.750 --> 00:36:37.790
the CEO is to understand how to draw that
line and when to draw that line.

00:36:37.790 --> 00:36:40.780
And, very frankly, it's very easy to

00:36:42.250 --> 00:36:46.560
articulate in a broad way that you are a
principled person.

00:36:48.500 --> 00:36:53.190
Where the rubber meets the road is how did
you act?

00:36:53.190 --> 00:36:55.070
So I'll give you a few examples.

00:37:00.630 --> 00:37:06.020
Five years ago, we did a competitive
analysis in the card business, and

00:37:06.020 --> 00:37:11.680
we said, what are all the drivers of
revenue that our competitors are using?

00:37:13.580 --> 00:37:20.220
And what we found was that there was a
substantial reliance on back end fees,

00:37:20.220 --> 00:37:21.910
and a range of practices that,

00:37:21.910 --> 00:37:27.090
frankly, would have generated a
substantial amount of money.

00:37:27.090 --> 00:37:33.170
And I'm being circumspect here but it was
very, very substantial.

00:37:34.210 --> 00:37:41.170
And and most of our competitors were in
fact, engaged in some

00:37:41.170 --> 00:37:46.190
of those practices and many were engaged
in, in most of them.

00:37:48.780 --> 00:37:53.570
We actually were very clear that we could
dramatically, even more, improve

00:37:53.570 --> 00:37:59.550
the financial performance of the company,
but, we said, we don't think that's right.

00:38:00.780 --> 00:38:03.623
And we said, and I said I don't think it's
right for

00:38:03.623 --> 00:38:08.213
the following reasons, is I developed when
I was running our card businesses,

00:38:08.213 --> 00:38:11.854
what we used in the company, we have three
operating principles.

00:38:11.854 --> 00:38:14.963
One is provide superior value for the
customers and

00:38:14.963 --> 00:38:16.993
clients that we choose to serve.

00:38:16.993 --> 00:38:22.614
Second, do it in a way that allows us to
achieve best in class economics,

00:38:22.614 --> 00:38:26.130
and every thing we do must support the
brand.

00:38:26.130 --> 00:38:28.070
Then we have a set of values for the
company.

00:38:28.070 --> 00:38:33.420
What was clear is, that one, we felt that
those practices were not consistent, and

00:38:33.420 --> 00:38:37.520
we articulated that to the team of why we
were not going to pursue it.

00:38:39.160 --> 00:38:41.730
We went through in general, and we went
through one by one.

00:38:43.000 --> 00:38:45.410
And we said this is, this is the trade
off.

00:38:45.410 --> 00:38:48.684
This is what we stand for as a company.

00:38:48.684 --> 00:38:57.800
And there are a number of examples where
you're faced with a situation

00:38:59.610 --> 00:39:06.420
where you know one, and these are the
toughest issues.

00:39:06.420 --> 00:39:06.920
It's legal.

00:39:09.350 --> 00:39:13.250
It's really not a problem, I mean so the
ethical, legal issues,

00:39:13.250 --> 00:39:15.720
I mean if you're gonna do something that's
wrong, you got a problem.

00:39:17.310 --> 00:39:23.170
But we have a situation where it's not,
it's perfectly legal.

00:39:24.200 --> 00:39:25.010
Everyone's doing it.

00:39:26.420 --> 00:39:26.920
Why not?

00:39:29.100 --> 00:39:31.410
And the question is, what is the
commitment?

00:39:31.410 --> 00:39:36.070
We were in a situation just this year.

00:39:36.070 --> 00:39:40.300
Every major financial services company
basically decided that

00:39:40.300 --> 00:39:41.620
they would cut their dividend.

00:39:43.220 --> 00:39:45.100
And, and we talked about it,

00:39:45.100 --> 00:39:48.770
and every, every banker that would come
in, investment banker, would say, Ken,

00:39:48.770 --> 00:39:51.340
everyone's doing it, there's really not
any pressure.

00:39:51.340 --> 00:39:55.570
And I said, look, you gotta understand,
with our company, we really have this

00:39:55.570 --> 00:40:01.090
concept of promises to keep, and unless I
thought

00:40:01.090 --> 00:40:06.630
that the company was really going under, I
just don't think we should do that.

00:40:06.630 --> 00:40:07.610
And that wasn't illegal.

00:40:07.610 --> 00:40:12.120
It wasn't, wouldn't be anything wrong,
morally, if I did that, but

00:40:12.120 --> 00:40:16.190
the question is, what were the values of
the company and

00:40:16.190 --> 00:40:18.210
what's the commitment that you need to
make?

00:40:18.210 --> 00:40:22.357
And I don't criticize another company for
cutting their dividend, but for

00:40:22.357 --> 00:40:25.155
us, I didn't think that was the right
thing to do.

00:40:25.155 --> 00:40:30.312
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:40:30.312 --> 00:40:36.619
&gt;&gt; I'm wondering in your 30 years at
American Express what you think you've,

00:40:36.619 --> 00:40:39.592
you did differently both early and

00:40:39.592 --> 00:40:44.276
later in your career in terms of both
within your job, and

00:40:44.276 --> 00:40:49.051
then within the, within the sphere of
doing things to sort of,

00:40:49.051 --> 00:40:55.310
further longer-term objectives that that
resulted in you being CEO today.

00:40:56.660 --> 00:41:01.770
&gt;&gt; You know, I think, and it's, I would
say it's more, it's been more that my

00:41:01.770 --> 00:41:07.340
behavior has evolved rather than a radical
change, but I'll give you a few examples

00:41:07.340 --> 00:41:13.540
that I think were helpful to me and what I
would caution with all advice,

00:41:13.540 --> 00:41:17.150
you've gotta personalize it and you've
gotta customize it to your own situation.

00:41:19.360 --> 00:41:25.070
One is what has always excited me is, I
always wanted to work with really bright

00:41:26.240 --> 00:41:31.860
people who I thought had good values and
would allow me to do exciting things.

00:41:33.660 --> 00:41:36.990
I never came to the company with the
intent that I would become CEO.

00:41:39.201 --> 00:41:44.680
Really it was until two or three years
before I became president, I remember

00:41:44.680 --> 00:41:48.300
Harvey saying to me, you know I think you
could run the company, it really was not,

00:41:48.300 --> 00:41:54.470
not that I was naive, but that just was
not the objective for me.

00:41:54.470 --> 00:42:03.430
And, and I think it's important that your
personal ambition does not overcome you.

00:42:03.430 --> 00:42:09.400
So that allowed me to be independent, to
take risks and

00:42:10.840 --> 00:42:14.970
help me develop a perspective that I
thought I was focused on.

00:42:14.970 --> 00:42:16.550
Here are thing that are not only right for

00:42:16.550 --> 00:42:18.629
the company, but here are things that I
wanna do.

00:42:19.760 --> 00:42:25.010
Doing them in a, in a, in a team concept
so I'm not a lone ranger, but

00:42:25.010 --> 00:42:29.140
at the same time not just focused on
myself.

00:42:30.700 --> 00:42:38.000
The second is that I've always believed
that you should focus on,

00:42:38.000 --> 00:42:45.170
what are some ideas that can really change
the status quo in the business?

00:42:45.170 --> 00:42:50.005
And and one of my first jobs which was a
lot of fun for me,

00:42:50.005 --> 00:42:54.750
it was a big risk, is we had a business
that sold merchandise through the mail.

00:42:55.840 --> 00:42:59.490
Now if you think about what's happening in
the online world: segmentation,

00:42:59.490 --> 00:43:04.970
information analysis, and whatever, this
was terrific preparation.

00:43:06.000 --> 00:43:06.840
But it was losing money,

00:43:07.890 --> 00:43:11.940
and no one who was regarded as high
potential wanted to go into that business.

00:43:13.190 --> 00:43:17.390
I actually thought that that channel was
exciting, and again, at that time,

00:43:17.390 --> 00:43:20.930
since I didn't believe I was gonna be at a
big company for a long time, I said

00:43:20.930 --> 00:43:26.250
this would be a great entrepreneurial
experience when I run my own business.

00:43:26.250 --> 00:43:29.770
So, let me get in and learn about this
direct marketing channel,

00:43:29.770 --> 00:43:34.420
because they sell a wide variety of
products, but we came up with some

00:43:35.970 --> 00:43:41.160
very innovative ideas in how to
merchandise, and in a two or

00:43:41.160 --> 00:43:43.560
three year period, we went from 100
million to 800 million in sales.

00:43:46.670 --> 00:43:49.400
And we developed some sourcing strategies.

00:43:49.400 --> 00:43:55.700
We went over to Asia and Japan and we had
a deal that I was able to work out with

00:43:55.700 --> 00:44:00.210
some of the top manufacturers, that they
would let us have a new product for 90 to

00:44:00.210 --> 00:44:06.295
120 days and we'd sort of tested for them,
and then they could go retail with it.

00:44:06.295 --> 00:44:11.390
And that worked out great for us, and
helped us get some

00:44:11.390 --> 00:44:16.246
decent margins because in that business
you're always pressed on the margin side.

00:44:16.246 --> 00:44:22.875
I also was very involved in the
development

00:44:22.875 --> 00:44:27.360
of our loyalty program, and saw the power
of

00:44:28.650 --> 00:44:34.194
what that could do, and, and rewards has
made a major difference for the company.

00:44:34.194 --> 00:44:38.982
So I, I like strategy a lot but I also
like to implement, but

00:44:38.982 --> 00:44:44.220
what's most important is I really like
changing the paradigm and

00:44:44.220 --> 00:44:47.088
I think that helped me tremendously.

00:44:47.088 --> 00:44:52.650
The third and I hesitate even saying this
because this can be self serving,

00:44:52.650 --> 00:44:56.137
but I think if you really wanna build
leadership,

00:44:56.137 --> 00:45:01.469
what's most important at the end of the
day, and the first thing I look for.

00:45:01.469 --> 00:45:04.872
And try to analyze with people is
integrity.

00:45:04.872 --> 00:45:08.330
And in my definition of integrity it's not
just being honest,

00:45:08.330 --> 00:45:11.278
it's what's the consistency of words and
actions.

00:45:11.278 --> 00:45:16.140
And over time what people saw in the
organization certainly is

00:45:16.140 --> 00:45:18.019
not that I was perfect.

00:45:18.019 --> 00:45:23.336
Because as I said, I would have strong
views if I thought sometimes at meetings,

00:45:23.336 --> 00:45:29.437
I've gotten better that their comments
were not particularly insightful.

00:45:29.437 --> 00:45:34.111
I would not necessarily listen carefully
to those

00:45:34.111 --> 00:45:38.295
comments I had been told I've improved a
lot.

00:45:38.295 --> 00:45:42.360
[LAUGH] in that area over time and I, and
I think I have.

00:45:43.480 --> 00:45:46.600
But what I did develop a reputation for

00:45:46.600 --> 00:45:50.330
in the company, was a consistency of words
and actions.

00:45:50.330 --> 00:45:54.170
And that helped me to develop a
followership and

00:45:54.170 --> 00:45:56.888
get the emotional engagement that you need
as a leader.

00:45:56.888 --> 00:46:01.335
&gt;&gt; Hi is this, [SOUND] okay, the concept
of

00:46:01.335 --> 00:46:06.547
constructive conflict really stuck out to
me and

00:46:06.547 --> 00:46:12.011
from the outside you can see the value of
conflict in

00:46:12.011 --> 00:46:17.117
situations but when you're actually
inside.

00:46:17.117 --> 00:46:20.808
And you're hearing negative ideas or
embracing that conflict,

00:46:20.808 --> 00:46:24.990
it can be really uncomfortable, and people
can get quite defensive.

00:46:24.990 --> 00:46:29.490
So I was just wondering how you have been
able to turn those negative thoughts and

00:46:29.490 --> 00:46:32.390
emotions into a positive aspect for

00:46:32.390 --> 00:46:35.590
the company and use it to really motivate
performance.

00:46:35.590 --> 00:46:43.080
Yeah, here's, and let me very clear, is
this is a ongoing effort in the company.

00:46:44.180 --> 00:46:49.160
And I would certainly not put us yet at
the level that I'd like to be at,

00:46:49.160 --> 00:46:53.160
cuz to your point, it is that hardest
thing to make happen.

00:46:55.140 --> 00:47:00.760
We're a company that I think is very
driven for results.

00:47:03.310 --> 00:47:07.280
But what we have always not done
historically and

00:47:07.280 --> 00:47:10.919
we've improved a lot in this, is to
confront each other.

00:47:12.030 --> 00:47:16.820
So what I say in AmEx speak, and I use
this often in the organization, that we,

00:47:16.820 --> 00:47:21.460
when we started off on this around ten
years ago, is I

00:47:21.460 --> 00:47:26.240
would say when you hear someone say at
AmEx, I don't mean to disagree with you.

00:47:26.240 --> 00:47:29.850
What they're really saying is, I totally
disagree with you.

00:47:29.850 --> 00:47:31.125
[LAUGH] I will annihilate you.

00:47:31.125 --> 00:47:34.690
[LAUGH] I will do everything in my power
to make sure this doesn't happen.

00:47:34.690 --> 00:47:40.770
So, that was extreme, but the reality is
that the sort of,

00:47:42.970 --> 00:47:50.430
behavior rules that were set up is I want
open and constructive confrontation.

00:47:50.430 --> 00:47:51.119
Here's what that means.

00:47:52.200 --> 00:47:54.500
You've got something to say, say it at the
meeting.

00:47:55.640 --> 00:48:00.460
And if it's outside the meeting and so,
the first few times when

00:48:00.460 --> 00:48:05.360
we put this in more than a few times,
you'd have sort of the usual

00:48:05.360 --> 00:48:10.220
behavior of people would have the meeting,
they'd go through issues, circle back.

00:48:10.220 --> 00:48:14.050
Ken, let me tell you, I really have a
concern with what so and so said.

00:48:15.740 --> 00:48:19.630
And I said, excuse me, let's call up so
and so, no, no, no, don't do it.

00:48:19.630 --> 00:48:20.760
We're gonna call them up.

00:48:20.760 --> 00:48:22.780
So and so, come in the office.

00:48:22.780 --> 00:48:23.665
So and so said.

00:48:23.665 --> 00:48:25.950
[LAUGH] Well, why didn't you say at the
meeting?

00:48:27.050 --> 00:48:27.780
And then I said, look,

00:48:27.780 --> 00:48:33.880
now let's respect each other, so what I
don't want when we disagree.

00:48:33.880 --> 00:48:35.800
That you necessarily say I think you're a
jerk.

00:48:37.384 --> 00:48:41.223
What you can say is look, I disagree with
the idea, but

00:48:41.223 --> 00:48:44.330
personal respect is critical.

00:48:44.330 --> 00:48:51.340
But don't hesitate to be very direct in
saying why you have the issue, how

00:48:51.340 --> 00:48:56.420
you're thinking about the issue, because
that's gonna bring out better ideas.

00:48:56.420 --> 00:49:02.110
And it is uncomfortable but I will tell
you, in every single area

00:49:03.110 --> 00:49:09.250
one of the most difficult things to
accomplish is the transition of CEOs.

00:49:11.510 --> 00:49:15.170
One is I did with Harvey, which was great
from a constructive confrontation.

00:49:15.170 --> 00:49:19.240
I said Harvey, let's take two day, and
let's go through a set of issues.

00:49:20.240 --> 00:49:22.150
What is it in the transition you're gonna
do.

00:49:22.150 --> 00:49:23.210
What am I gonna do?

00:49:23.210 --> 00:49:25.580
What do we agree on, what do we not?

00:49:26.810 --> 00:49:29.770
And sometimes those sessions get heated.

00:49:29.770 --> 00:49:34.790
Sometimes, but the end of the day was done
with really very strong

00:49:34.790 --> 00:49:38.997
personal directness and

00:49:38.997 --> 00:49:44.370
the behaviour was very well done.

00:49:45.530 --> 00:49:48.960
So I think you can be direct, I think you
can be honest.

00:49:48.960 --> 00:49:50.200
I think you can raise your voice.

00:49:51.280 --> 00:49:54.449
And part of the counseling I give to
people as they go through is.

00:49:55.950 --> 00:50:01.130
Sometimes, you may wanna telegraph, and
I'll say to someone, you know, I'm gonna,

00:50:01.130 --> 00:50:09.415
I really feel strongly about this, so I am
gonna argue in a very vociferous way.

00:50:09.415 --> 00:50:13.480
[LAUGH] Don't take it personally, but

00:50:13.480 --> 00:50:17.140
understand, this is something that is
really meaningful.

00:50:18.420 --> 00:50:26.622
And so what I what I don't want to leave
you with the impression with is that

00:50:28.360 --> 00:50:33.010
constructive confrontation is taking place
everywhere and is at the level but

00:50:33.010 --> 00:50:39.440
is something that I am relentless about in
the organization because you,

00:50:39.440 --> 00:50:41.660
you waste so much time.

00:50:41.660 --> 00:50:46.340
I mean, if we're, everything is moving so
fast paced in this world, that if you

00:50:46.340 --> 00:50:50.950
had people saying, another phrase we used
to use is, let's take this offline.

00:50:50.950 --> 00:50:53.043
What do you mean take it offline?

00:50:53.043 --> 00:50:54.678
[LAUGH] We're right here.

00:50:54.678 --> 00:50:55.570
Take it offline?

00:50:55.570 --> 00:50:57.350
Let's talk about it right here.

00:50:57.350 --> 00:51:00.950
So it's, it's, it's very very important.

00:51:00.950 --> 00:51:03.210
The more you can get people engaged.

00:51:03.210 --> 00:51:04.920
And talking through the issues.

00:51:04.920 --> 00:51:11.030
What it actually says is that I respect
you and your ideas, and the fact that

00:51:11.030 --> 00:51:15.210
you are impassioned about those ideas, and
you're willing to take some risk.

00:51:15.210 --> 00:51:19.390
Because that's what it takes in business
is, you gotta put some risk out there.

00:51:19.390 --> 00:51:23.690
If you're gonna be careful, then we're not
gonna move forward as a company,

00:51:23.690 --> 00:51:24.460
we're not gonna be a leader.

00:51:25.960 --> 00:51:29.123
&gt;&gt; Last question.

00:51:29.123 --> 00:51:32.210
&gt;&gt; Hi, my name Regina Cross and I'm an MBA
one.

00:51:32.210 --> 00:51:34.940
I had a question with respect to some of
the learning that you

00:51:34.940 --> 00:51:37.010
have with the BRIC countries.

00:51:37.010 --> 00:51:40.310
You mentioned that you're focused on the
traditions and culture and history.

00:51:40.310 --> 00:51:42.560
And just wondered, kind of, maybe, what
the top one or

00:51:42.560 --> 00:51:45.900
two things are that you have learned so
far with your research.

00:51:45.900 --> 00:51:49.530
And how you are working within your
organization to meet the needs.

00:51:49.530 --> 00:51:52.630
And the, the, the variances within the
culture in order to meet their needs?

00:51:52.630 --> 00:51:59.590
&gt;&gt; Here's two or three, and some of these
are pretty basic, but, there

00:51:59.590 --> 00:52:06.430
is a strong view, that the U.S. looks down
on everyone, and can be patronizing.

00:52:08.910 --> 00:52:14.580
And the issue that I think is important,
and one of the things that I learned is,

00:52:14.580 --> 00:52:20.720
in fact, if you're meeting someone and
people really believe,

00:52:20.720 --> 00:52:26.620
and you tell them I actually read a book
about your country.

00:52:27.960 --> 00:52:31.320
I read some history about it, and I took a
step back, I found and

00:52:31.320 --> 00:52:33.868
say, wow that's pretty unusual [LAUGH].

00:52:33.868 --> 00:52:39.090
I mean, really, it's, it's I just came
back from a week trip in the in

00:52:39.090 --> 00:52:45.619
the the Mideast and went to a number of
countries.

00:52:46.910 --> 00:52:49.420
And I wasn't able to read up on every one.

00:52:51.010 --> 00:52:56.820
But whether it was a dinner meeting or
lunch meeting letting someone

00:52:56.820 --> 00:53:01.250
know that I knew something about the
politics of the country, I knew something

00:53:01.250 --> 00:53:06.210
about the economic environment, I may have
known a particular history point.

00:53:09.160 --> 00:53:12.500
Lets people know, just as you're forming,

00:53:15.680 --> 00:53:21.410
some type of personal engagement, that you
take them seriously.

00:53:21.410 --> 00:53:26.860
And that you think that their country is
important and they even learn something.

00:53:26.860 --> 00:53:33.250
And so the biggest loaning in a sense to
me is, just as you would any

00:53:33.250 --> 00:53:39.170
business problem, to go into a country,
and you're running off the plane and

00:53:39.170 --> 00:53:42.180
you might say to someone, look, brief me
about a few things that are happening

00:53:42.180 --> 00:53:46.890
here, is not exactly gonna endear you to
the people that you meet with

00:53:46.890 --> 00:53:50.640
cause they're going to say it's all
superficial, we know they're about.

00:53:50.640 --> 00:53:54.370
This is the deal, but if they say, boy
this person actually took time.

00:53:55.990 --> 00:54:00.410
and, and there's no problem admitting
that, look I'm a neofight,

00:54:02.460 --> 00:54:06.770
I don't have, I've only been to the
country once, or twice, or whatever.

00:54:06.770 --> 00:54:10.690
But to show real engagement, and.

00:54:11.920 --> 00:54:18.870
Remember giving some feedback to one of

00:54:18.870 --> 00:54:26.789
our people where we went into a market and
the person wanted to take

00:54:26.789 --> 00:54:32.825
him to a place that he thought was very
special in his country.

00:54:32.825 --> 00:54:39.350
The person has said I think I'll go back
to the hotel it's been a long trip,

00:54:39.350 --> 00:54:44.570
little bit tired, I don't think that helps
[LAUGH] build the type of

00:54:44.570 --> 00:54:47.892
relationship and engagement that you want.

00:54:47.892 --> 00:54:48.680
&gt;&gt; Thank you very much.

00:54:48.680 --> 00:54:49.214
Really appreciated.

00:54:49.214 --> 00:54:55.090
&gt;&gt; Thank you.
